From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000006 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Sheinberg <David_Sheinberg@brown.edu>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 16:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] ptp: netc: program the clock when scaled_ppm is zero

netc_timer_adjfine() returned success without touching the hardware when
scaled_ppm was zero.  The adjustment is ABSOLUTE -- new_period is always
computed from priv->base_period, which is set once at probe and never
updated -- so scaled_ppm == 0 is a meaningful request meaning "run at the
nominal rate", not "nothing to do".

Because the call reported success, the PTP core cached the value
(ptp->dialed_frequency = tx->freq in ptp_clock_adjtime()), so every
later query answered 0 while NETC_TMR_ADD still held the old period.
The clock silently ran at the previous rate and every tool reported it
as nominal.

Measured on FRDM-IMX95-15x15, against CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW over 40 s:

  set -150000 ppb  ->  -149.9992 ppm   (correct)
  set       0      ->  -150.0011 ppm   (ignored, reports 0)
  set       0      ->  -150.0044 ppm   (still ignored)
  set      +1 ppb  ->    +0.0022 ppm   (correct -- +1 works, 0 does not)

The practical damage is that the divergence is permanent.  linuxptp
recovers from a bad read with

    fadj = clockadj_get_freq(); clockadj_set_freq(fadj);

so once the cache reads 0 and the hardware does not, every restart writes
0 back -- precisely the write that is dropped -- and nothing self-heals.
phc2sys then hunts against a stale rate instead of locking.

Upstream commit 87a201d ("ptp: netc: add NETC V4 Timer PTP driver
support") has no such early return, and nxp-imx/linux-imx lf-6.18.y does
not either; only the older BSP branches (lf-6.6.y, lf-6.12.y) carry it.
This drops it, matching upstream.

With the clock at nominal, phc2sys locks at +/-28 ns as grandmaster.

Signed-off-by: David Sheinberg <David_Sheinberg@brown.edu>
---
 drivers/ptp/ptp_netc.c | 3 ---
 1 file changed, 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/ptp/ptp_netc.c b/drivers/ptp/ptp_netc.c
--- a/drivers/ptp/ptp_netc.c
+++ b/drivers/ptp/ptp_netc.c
@@ -458,9 +458,6 @@
 	struct netc_timer *priv = ptp_to_netc_timer(ptp);
 	u64 new_period;

-	if (!scaled_ppm)
-		return 0;
-
 	new_period = adjust_by_scaled_ppm(priv->base_period, scaled_ppm);
 	netc_timer_adjust_period(priv, new_period);

